1. Introduction

1.1 From AI Governance to the Governance of an Intelligence-Integrated Civilization

The dominant framing of AI policy is AI governance: how should humanity regulate artificial intelligence? This framing treats AI as an external object—a technology to be bounded, steered, or constrained by institutions that remain essentially unchanged.

This framing is becoming inadequate. In the emerging regime, artificial intelligence is not an external object but an infrastructural substrate. It is becoming:

· Scientific infrastructure: hypothesis generation, experimental design, literature synthesis, simulation.
· Administrative infrastructure: eligibility determination, permitting, inspection, correspondence.
· Economic infrastructure: pricing, matching, logistics, risk assessment.
· Simulation infrastructure: climate modeling, epidemiological forecasting, policy simulation.
· Decision infrastructure: option generation, consequence estimation, anomaly detection.
· Spatial infrastructure: navigation, resource management, life-support coordination in off-Earth settlements.
· Discovery infrastructure: materials science, drug discovery, mathematics.

When intelligence becomes infrastructure, the governance question changes. It is no longer:

How should humanity regulate artificial intelligence?

It becomes:

How does a civilization govern itself when intelligence becomes infrastructure?

This is a broader and more difficult question. Regulation presupposes a regulator sufficiently distinct from the regulated to observe, evaluate, and constrain it. Infrastructure, by contrast, is embedded in the operations of the governed. A civilization whose cognitive, scientific, administrative, and spatial infrastructure is increasingly artificial must develop institutions capable of maintaining stable coupling with that infrastructure—not merely controlling it from outside.

1.2 The Institutional Synchronization Problem

Human civilization has traditionally evolved within a relatively narrow cognitive and institutional regime. Laws, administrations, scientific institutions, economic systems, and democratic processes operate through cycles measured in months, years, and generations. The biological and organizational substrates of governance impose hard constraints on the rate at which institutions can perceive, deliberate, decide, and revise.

Artificial intelligence introduces a different temporal regime. Frontier AI systems can operate continuously, process vast information quantities, coordinate multiple computational processes, and interact with digital environments at speeds inaccessible to individual humans. Recent empirical work on task-completion time horizons indicates that frontier models are extending the length of autonomously completable tasks at a doubling period of approximately four months (METR, 2025; Epoch AI, 2025). This rate is not merely faster than human institutional cycles; it is qualitatively different in kind from the rates for which modern governance architectures were designed.

The central challenge is therefore not regulation in the conventional sense. It is institutional synchronization: the maintenance of a stable, legitimate, and reversible coupling between human institutions and an intelligence whose operational tempo exceeds the tempo for which those institutions were designed.

The theoretical core of this paper is the following condition:

\boxed{\text{Stable Coupling} \neq \text{Equal Speed}} \tag{1}

An institution does not need to become as fast as the intelligence it governs. It must remain capable of maintaining a stable relationship with that intelligence—perceiving relevant changes, verifying claims, deciding legitimately, enforcing effectively, and revising when evidence changes. Equal speed is neither necessary nor sufficient for stable coupling. What is required is sufficient adaptive capacity relative to the rate of change, not matched capacity.

This distinction is fundamental. It reframes the problem from a race (which institutions would lose) to a synchronization problem (which institutions can, in principle, solve).

1.3 Scope and Falsifiability

Scope. This framework is a theoretical specification, not an empirical validation. It proposes diagnostic quantities, architectural principles, and institutional mechanisms. It does not claim that the PAI has been implemented, that its thresholds have been calibrated against real institutional data, or that its anti-capture safeguards have been tested against the full capture taxonomy. The equations are formal; the metrics are operationalizable in principle; the worked example (§8) is explicitly synthetic.

What the framework claims:

1. That institutional synchronization is a distinct and tractable problem, separable from AI regulation.
2. That stable coupling does not require equal speed, and that the Synchronization Ratio $\Sigma$ and Synchronization Margin $M_S$ provide meaningful diagnostics of coupling adequacy.
3. That the PAI architecture, drawing on VSM, dual control, and polycentric governance, can in principle maintain stable coupling under Regime C conditions.
4. That human sovereignty requires specific institutional safeguards (anti-capture architecture, preference-formation sovereignty, constitutional constraints) that do not follow automatically from synchronization capacity.

What the framework does not claim:

1. That any specific threshold value ($\theta_\Sigma$, $\theta_O$, parity thresholds) is empirically validated.
2. That the PAI is immune to capture or governance inversion.
3. That the multiplanetary transition criteria are derived from settlement data rather than expert judgment.
4. That the framework is complete; it is a specification of the problem and a set of candidate mechanisms.

Falsifiability. The framework would be falsified if:

· Institutions in Regime C ($\Sigma \gg 1$) consistently maintained stable coupling without structural intervention (contradicting the Regime C → structural intervention claim).
· The anti-capture safeguards proved systematically ineffective against the dominant capture mechanisms in empirical testing.
· Multiplanetary settlements demonstrated effective Earth-based governance above the criticality threshold specified in §9.
· The Oversight Feasibility Function failed to predict oversight failure in domains where it was expected to operate.

1.4 Intellectual Context and Novel Contribution

This framework builds upon five decades of governance theory addressing technology-society timing mismatches. We position our contribution within this literature rather than presenting familiar concepts as novel discoveries.

The Pacing Problem. Larry Downes (2009) coined the "pacing problem" to describe the structural mismatch between exponential technological change and incremental legal-social adaptation. Gary Marchant's edited volume The Growing Gap Between Emerging Technologies and Legal-Ethical Oversight (2011) provides the standard reference, documenting how regulatory institutions systematically lag behind technological development.

The Collingridge Dilemma. David Collingridge (1980) articulated the structural dilemma of technology regulation: early intervention occurs when control is easy but understanding is limited; late intervention occurs when understanding is rich but control is difficult.

Anticipatory Governance. David Guston's framework (2014) proposes "a broad-based capacity extended through society that can act on a variety of inputs to manage emerging knowledge-based technologies while such management is still possible." Its components—foresight, integration, engagement—prefigure the adaptive loops we propose.

Adaptive Governance. Folke, Olsson, and colleagues developed adaptive governance theory for "polycentric, nested, quasi-autonomous organisations" managing rapid change and high uncertainty through experimentation and social learning. A central insight—particularly Olsson et al.'s "Shooting the Rapids" (2006)—is that transitions to adaptive governance emerge from crisis and are prepared by emergent leadership and shadow networks before crisis. The PAI framework incorporates this precondition.

Polycentric Governance. Elinor Ostrom's polycentric governance framework describes "multiple governing authorities at different scales rather than a monocentric unit," each possessing genuine autonomy and linked through information and monitoring systems.

Cybernetics and Viable Systems. Ashby's Law of Requisite Variety (1956) and Beer's Viable System Model (1972, 1979, 1985) provide the formal cybernetic foundation for institutional parity and adaptive architecture.

Dual Control. Feldbaum (1960) and Wittenmark (1975) established dual control theory, in which a controller must simultaneously regulate a system and learn its dynamics, creating an exploration-exploitation trade-off. Holmström (2026) has proposed applying dual control to governance learning.

The Novel Contribution. What distinguishes this framework is the specification of institutional synchronization requirements under a speed regime where AI capability growth may exceed the rate for which any existing governance framework was designed. We contribute: (i) a formal distinction between institutional synchronization and institutional speed-matching; (ii) synchronization regimes and the Synchronization Margin as diagnostic concepts, with domain-specific calibration; (iii) an anti-capture architecture with enforcement mechanisms and a systematic stress test against the capture taxonomy; (iv) a polycentric multiplanetary architecture with derived transition criteria; and (v) an explicit failure-modes analysis with diagnostic signals. The framing shift from "AI governance" to "governance of an intelligence-integrated civilization" is itself a contribution: it changes what counts as a governance problem.

