2. Related Work

2.1 The Pacing Problem and the Collingridge Dilemma

The pacing problem describes the structural mismatch between the rate of technological change and the rate of legal-institutional adaptation. Downes (2009) and Marchant (2011) documented this mismatch across multiple domains: biotechnology, nanotechnology, information technology. The AI case is continuous with this literature but distinguished by the acceleration of the mismatch itself: capability doubling periods have shortened from approximately seven months (2019–2024) to approximately four months (2024–2026) in the measured autonomous task-completion horizon .

The Collingridge dilemma (1980) specifies the structural trap: early intervention is feasible but uninformed; late intervention is informed but costly. The PAI framework addresses this trap through modular, reversible rules with capability-triggered specificity (§4.6).

2.2 Anticipatory, Adaptive, and Polycentric Governance

Anticipatory governance (Guston, 2014) proposes capacities for foresight, integration, and engagement that operate before technological entrenchment. It is a precursor to the PAI's forward-scanning function (System 4 in VSM terms).

Adaptive governance (Folke; Olsson; Gunderson) emphasizes experimentation, learning, and polycentric collaboration under conditions of high uncertainty. Its central empirical finding—that adaptive governance transitions require shadow networks and emergent leadership before crisis—has direct implications for PAI cultivation (§4.5) .

Polycentric governance (Ostrom, 2010) describes systems of multiple, overlapping, quasi-autonomous authorities. It is the appropriate framework for multiplanetary governance, where no single authority can legitimately or operationally govern across communication delays .

2.3 Cybernetics: Requisite Variety and Viable Systems

Ashby's Law of Requisite Variety (1956) states that for a control system to be stable, the controller's variety must be at least equal to the variety of the system being controlled. "Only variety can absorb variety." This is the formal basis of the Institutional Parity Principle (§3.2).

Beer's Viable System Model (1972, 1979, 1985) specifies five recursive subsystems (operations, coordination, operational management, intelligence, policy) plus an algedonic channel for emergency bypass. It is the formal basis of the PAI architecture (§4) .

2.4 Dual Control and Governance Learning

Dual control theory (Feldbaum, 1960; Wittenmark, 1975) addresses systems in which the controller must simultaneously regulate and learn the system's dynamics. Every intervention is simultaneously an action and an experiment. This creates an exploration-exploitation trade-off: probing actions may reduce short-term performance but improve future control .

Holmström (2026) has recently proposed applying dual control to governance learning. We adopt this framing while grounding it in the established dual-control literature.

2.5 Positioning

The PAI framework is not a replacement for these literatures but a specialization. It takes the pacing problem as the structural diagnosis, adaptive governance as the methodological orientation, polycentric governance as the spatial architecture, cybernetics as the formal foundation, and dual control as the learning mechanism. Its contribution is the specification of these elements under the condition of a qualitatively different speed ratio—one in which the pace of capability change exceeds not merely institutional cycles but the rate for which any existing governance design assumption was calibrated.

