5. Human Sovereignty as Boundary Condition

5.1 The Authority-Capability Distinction

Parallel adaptation must not become institutional delegation of civilization to artificial intelligence. A dangerous interpretation would be:

\text{AI} \rightarrow \text{Institution} \rightarrow \text{Humanity} \tag{11}

where AI gradually becomes the effective decision-maker. The proposed architecture requires:

\text{Humanity} \rightarrow \text{Institution} \leftrightarrow \text{AI} \tag{12}

Artificial intelligence may provide analysis, simulation, forecasting, policy alternatives, anomaly detection, and institutional diagnostics. Legitimate political authority remains human.

\text{Authority}_{\text{human}} \neq \text{Capability}_{\text{AI}} \tag{13}

A system can possess greater analytical capability without possessing legitimate authority.

Connection to Anti-Capture Architecture. Equation 13 is not self-enforcing. It is preserved only if the institution can independently verify AI-generated claims and if the anti-capture safeguards of §6 are operative. Without these, Equation 13 is a formal statement without a mechanism.

5.2 The Moral Agency Framework

Schmitz and Bryson (2026) propose a Moral Agency Framework (MAF) for legitimate AI integration in bureaucracies:

(a) Clear human accountability. No AI system enters the decision loop without a named human accountable officer.

(b) Verification capacity. Humans whose work is augmented must be able to verify system correctness.

(c) Non-inhibition of legitimacy and stewardship. AI is introduced only where it does not inhibit the bureaucracy's twin aims.

Measurement. Ethics sink incidence—the count of decisions where the accountability chain cannot be traced to a named human—should be measured pre/post AI integration.

5.3 Triadic Legitimacy Model

Democratic legitimacy for AI-integrated governance requires:

1. Transparency/Explainability: Decisions traceable to comprehensible reasoning.
2. Human Oversight/Accountability: Named humans bear responsibility.
3. Public Engagement/Inclusion: Affected communities have meaningful participation.

Taiwan's vTaiwan platform demonstrates AI-enhanced deliberation that preserves legitimacy: AI synthesizes citizen input while human legislature retains authority.

5.4 Preference-Formation Sovereignty

The authority-capability distinction addresses who decides, but not who shapes what is decidable. AI can influence human preferences through persuasion, recommendation, agenda-setting, and information filtering. Research on the "autonomy paradox" identifies a tension: AI systems can reduce barriers to participation while simultaneously weakening the independence of political judgment, or support reflective preference formation while constraining citizens' ability to act on those preferences .

Preference-Formation Sovereignty. The capacity of a polity to form preferences through processes that are not systematically manipulated by AI systems whose optimization targets are exogenous to democratic deliberation.

Operational mechanisms:

· Transparency of AI-mediated political communication. Disclosure requirements for algorithmic curation of political content.
· Limits on AI-generated persuasion in democratic processes. Restrictions on synthetic media in electoral contexts.
· Independent audit of recommender systems. Public audit of algorithmic amplification of political content.
· Constitutional review of AI-integrated administrative decisions. Judicial oversight of AI-augmented decision-making.

Preference-formation sovereignty is the dimension most vulnerable to epistemic capture through discourse influence, and the least developed in current governance frameworks.

