6. Anti-Capture Architecture

6.1 Epistemic Capture as Primary Threat

Classic regulatory capture involves industry influencing its regulators through lobbying, revolving doors, and campaign contributions. AI governance faces an additional mechanism: epistemic capture.

Epistemic capture occurs when a governing institution becomes dependent on the entities it governs for the knowledge required to govern them. A government that cannot independently understand AI cannot meaningfully choose policy—it can only ratify what it is told.

6.2 Mechanisms and Taxonomy

Research on "Big AI" regulatory capture identifies 27 mechanisms across five categories, validated through manual annotation of 100 news articles around four global AI events between 2023 and 2025 . The analysis identifies 249 instances of capture mechanisms. The most recurring categories are:

· Discourse and Epistemic Influence: Industry shapes the categories through which AI risks are understood. Dominant rationalizing narratives are "regulation stifles innovation," "red tape," "national interest," and "competitiveness" .
· Elusion of Law: Violations and contentious interpretations of antitrust, privacy, copyright, and labor law.
· Lobbying and Revolving Doors: Former policymakers advising AI companies; political donations .
· Government Complicity: EU Commission President calling for deregulation; General Purpose AI Code of Practice diluted at each stage .

6.3 Four Structural Safeguards

Safeguard 1: Structural Separation.
Observation and evaluation functions must be institutionally independent from deployment and procurement. Operationalized: Physically separate observation into a publicly funded, non-industry lab with subpoena power to access model internals and training data. Funding: Dedicated public appropriation, not industry contracts. Authority: Statutory subpoena power for model internals, training data, and deployment logs.

Safeguard 2: Adversarial Review.
Independent red teams with access to model internals and training data. Operationalized: Institutional AI governance graph approach: public immutable manifest declaring legal states, transitions, and sanctions, with Oracle/Controller runtime attaching enforceable consequences. Access rights: Statutory right of access to model internals under appropriate confidentiality protections.

Safeguard 3: Epistemic Parity.
No AI system's outputs shall be treated as institutionally valid knowledge unless the epistemic categories used to generate those outputs have been subject to contestation by affected communities through a process that does not require those communities to translate their knowledge into a dominant schema. Operationalized: Dual epistemology—every capability evaluation must have independent evaluation by a non-industry evaluator using different scaffolding. Procedural test: who can contest; what counts as contestation; what access rights exist; what happens when the institution cannot independently reproduce a claim.

Safeguard 4: Personnel Firewalls.
Cooling-off periods and epistemic disclosure—not just financial, but model access disclosure. Personnel must disclose which proprietary models they accessed in prior 24 months. Enforcement: Disclosure requirements with civil penalties; cooling-off periods of 24–36 months for senior personnel.

