7. Institutional Synchronization Dashboard

7.1 Capability Observatory

The Capability Observatory tracks:

· METR task horizon (50% and 80%), with scaffolding conditions reported. As of early 2026, frontier models achieve approximately 14.5 hours at 50% and 1 hour at 80% .
· Epoch Capabilities Index (ECI) as general index, with explicit acknowledgment of domain-specialization limitations. ECI is calibrated with Claude 3.5 Sonnet at 130 and GPT-5 at 150 . The ECI frontier has advanced linearly by 14 points per year since the introduction of reasoning models . Open-weight models lag frontier closed models by approximately four months (8 ECI points) .
· Domain-specific benchmarks.
· Deployed inference budgets. Inference scaling can extend task horizons without architectural improvement ; the Observatory must track inference budgets per evaluation.

Scaffolding artifact. METR's 50% vs 80% horizons diverge substantially. Scaffolding choices affect measured capability. The Observatory must report capability ranges with scaffolding conditions, not point estimates.

7.2 Institutional Responsiveness Observatory

Tracks:

· $T_I(d)$ per domain.
· Adaptive Capacity Wheel scores (22 criteria, 6 dimensions: variety, learning capacity, room for autonomous change, leadership, availability of resources, fair governance) .
· Epistemic independence metrics (§6.5).
· Ethics sink incidence (§5.2).

7.3 Parity Vector

\mathbf{P}(d) = (p_1, p_2, p_3, p_4, p_5, p_6) \tag{14}

· $p_1$: detection latency
· $p_2$: verification capacity
· $p_3$: enforcement latency
· $p_4$: revision latency
· $p_5$: legitimacy maintenance
· $p_6$: independent epistemic capacity

Parity condition:

p_i(d) \geq \theta_i(d) \quad \forall i \tag{15}

Thresholds $\theta_i(d)$ are domain-specific and calibrated through the same protocol as $\theta_\Sigma$ (§3.4).

7.4 Oversight Feasibility

$O_f$ per Equation 8, reported with components $H_{AI}$, $H_H$, $X$, $D$, $V$.

7.5 Synchronization Margin

$M_S(d)$ per Equation 4, reported with $\Sigma(d)$ and $\theta_\Sigma(d)$.

The dashboard is not an external reporting instrument. It is implemented as a governance graph: an immutable, auditable manifest of capability states, institutional states, thresholds, and transitions, with a runtime controller that attaches consequences. This connects the dashboard to the anti-capture architecture: the same infrastructure that monitors capability also enforces verification requirements.

