9. Multiplanetary Extension

9.1 The Asymmetry of Terrestrial and Off-Earth Governance

On Earth, the emerging architecture is:

\text{Earth} \rightarrow \text{AI} \rightarrow \text{Institution} \tag{20}

AI capabilities are deployed, institutions respond. Even in Regime C, the institution operates in the same communication regime as the capability.

On Mars, or in any off-Earth settlement beyond light-delay thresholds, the architecture is different:

\text{Mars} \rightarrow \text{AI} \leftrightarrow \text{Local Institution} \tag{21}

Communication latency imposes local autonomy. A signal to Mars can take roughly 4 to 24 minutes one way, depending on orbital positions . A settlement cannot wait for decisions that affect survival. The local institution must be capable of autonomous operation, and its interaction with AI is direct rather than mediated by Earth.

This asymmetry is not merely a communication-engineering problem. It is a governance-architecture problem: local institutions must be capable of maintaining stable coupling with AI without external support, under conditions where the consequences of failure are survival-critical.

9.2 Formalizing Required Local Autonomy

We formalize required local autonomy as:

A_L = \alpha \cdot D_c + \beta \cdot V_d + \gamma \cdot C_s \tag{22}

where:

· $D_c$ = communication delay (one-way light delay, normalized)
· $V_d$ = local decision velocity (rate at which decisions must be made, normalized)
· $C_s$ = local criticality/survival dependence (normalized 0–1)
· $\alpha, \beta, \gamma$ = domain-specific weights, with $\alpha + \beta + \gamma = 1$

Monotonicity conditions:

\frac{\partial A_L}{\partial D_c} > 0, \quad \frac{\partial A_L}{\partial V_d} > 0, \quad \frac{\partial A_L}{\partial C_s} > 0 \tag{23}

Higher communication delay, higher decision velocity, and higher criticality all increase required local autonomy.

Implication. Earth-based governance of Martian settlements is not merely inefficient. It is structurally impossible for decisions above a criticality threshold. The PAI framework must therefore be instantiated locally, with Earth retaining only protocol-level coordination (spectrum, registration, rescue, trade standards).

9.3 Bifurcation and Private Actors

As of 2026, lunar governance is bifurcating:

· Artemis Accords: U.S.-led, 67 signatories (May 2026), including 5 African nations . By July 2026, Serbia became the 69th signatory and Mauritius the 70th .
· ILRS: China-Russia led, approximately 13 partner nations including Belarus, Pakistan, Egypt, South Africa, and Venezuela . Thailand, Senegal, and Serbia have signed both frameworks .

Safety Zones under both frameworks create exclusionary territorial claims that test OST non-appropriation. Both claim to uphold OST while offering divergent interpretations of resource rights.

Private actors complicate state-centric frameworks further. SpaceX's Mars claims and the Martian Federalist proposal for absolute city-state autonomy directly challenge any Layer 3 Charter that claims universal authority .

Resolution. We treat OST Article II as a contested but operative constraint. The PAI does not assume a single interpretation; it treats non-appropriation as a coordination problem to be solved through the dispute-resolution layer (§9.6).

9.4 Polycentric Architecture

Layer 1 — Local Settlement PAI. Autonomous decision latency less than light-delay. AI as institutional infrastructure within MAF constraints. Self-governance transition via quantified criteria (§9.5).

Layer 2 — Interplanetary Coordination Protocol. Protocol layer, not government:

· Spectrum management as foundational (Martian spectrum chaos jeopardizes survival).
· Registration and rescue non-negotiable (OST Art. V, VIII).
· Safety zones as coordination, not appropriation: time-limited, purpose-bound, mandatory disclosure, reciprocal visitation.

Layer 3 — Civilizational Charter. Minimal hard constraints: non-appropriation (contested), human sovereignty, anti-domination. Does not claim universal authority over non-consenting settlements.

This three-layer architecture maps onto Ostrom's polycentric governance framework, which envisions "multiple governing authorities at different scales rather than a monocentric unit" .

