10. Failure Modes

The framework generates a taxonomy of failure modes. These are not hypothetical; they are the conditions under which the PAI's mechanisms would fail.

10.1 Governance Inversion. Formalization reduces operational coherence when formal rules become more complex than the system they govern, or when compliance displaces substantive governance. The PAI mitigates through capability-triggered review (P4), algedonic bypass (Eq. 9), and vector-valued parity (Eq. 15). Inversion remains a live risk.

10.2 Epistemic Capture. The institution loses independent knowledge-generation capacity and becomes dependent on the entities it governs. Mitigated by §6.3 safeguards; monitored by §6.5 metrics.

10.3 Algedonic Overload. Every signal becomes emergency; the bypass channel saturates. Mitigated by the multidimensional Oversight Feasibility Function (Eq. 8), which prevents duration alone from triggering algedonic activation.

10.4 Legitimacy Drift. Adaptation outpaces democratic authorization. The institution remains capable but loses legitimacy. Mitigated by TLM and preference-formation sovereignty mechanisms (§5.3–5.4).

10.5 Institutional Obsolescence. The institution remains legitimate but loses adaptive capacity, becoming a ceremonial body while effective governance migrates elsewhere. This is the endpoint of Regime C without intervention.

10.6 Synchronization Collapse. $M_S \leq 1$ across multiple domains simultaneously, indicating system-wide structural mismatch. This is the condition the framework is designed to prevent.

Each failure mode has diagnostic signals and mitigation mechanisms. Empirical validation of these mechanisms is a research priority (R6).

