12. Research Program

The framework generates eight research problems:

R1 — Measurement. How should capability velocity be measured without conflating task-horizon with general intelligence? METR as primary metric; ECI as composite; domain-specific observatories required. Benchmark ceiling and scaffolding artifacts require explicit reporting.

R2 — Synchronization. How can institutions detect when adaptive capacity is falling behind? Dual dashboards with $\Sigma$, $M_S$, parity vector, and oversight feasibility. Calibration of $\theta_\Sigma$ against historical institutional failure data.

R3 — Governance. How can AI participate in institutional reasoning without acquiring illegitimate authority? MAF and TLM pilot testing; ethics sink incidence measurement.

R4 — Adaptation. How can laws evolve continuously while preserving democratic legitimacy? Democracy Levels framework and vTaiwan model as experimental conditions; capability-triggered review as mechanism.

R5 — Multiplanetary Extension. How should synchronization mechanisms operate across communication delays? Extension of Agent Mars (Wang, 2026) simulation with governance and legitimacy layers. Empirical calibration of transition criteria.

R6 — Failure Modes. Do the mitigation mechanisms actually mitigate? Comparative study of institutions with varying formalization, capture exposure, and adaptive capacity.

R7 — Preference-Formation Sovereignty. How can a polity maintain sovereignty over preference formation when AI mediates political communication? Audit mechanisms and constitutional review as experimental conditions.

R8 — Recursive Governance. How should the PAI govern its own use of AI? Reflexive audit as a research condition. The institution that governs AI is itself increasingly AI-integrated.

