13. Limitations

False precision. The equations in this paper look quantitative, but many parameters are not yet calibrated or measurable. $\theta_\Sigma$ and $\theta_O$ are provisional. The Oversight Feasibility Function's functional form is a design choice, not an empirically derived law. The framework is a specification of the problem and a set of candidate mechanisms, not a validated predictive model.

Measurement gaps. The Capability Observatory relies on METR and ECI, which measure task horizon and benchmark performance, not general intelligence. Domain-specific capability measurement is immature. Institutional responsiveness metrics require data many institutions do not collect.

Legitimacy risks. The PAI architecture could itself become a technocratic institution insulated from democratic contestation. The anti-capture safeguards are necessary but not sufficient. Preference-formation sovereignty is the least developed dimension.

Capture risks. The anti-capture stress test (§6.4) reveals that the four safeguards cover approximately 60% of the 27 capture mechanisms. Elusion of law requires external enforcement capacity outside the PAI's scope. Narrative capture via research funding requires additional safeguards.

Multiplanetary uncertainties. The transition criteria are derived from expert judgment, not settlement data. The reciprocal recognition protocol risks collusion. The bifurcation of lunar governance creates coordination problems that no single framework can resolve.

Ordinality limits. The Institutional Speed Gap $G_I$ is reclassified as a conceptual diagnostic because log-derivatives of ordinal quantities lack quantitative interpretation. The quantitative work is done by $\Sigma$ and $M_S$, which are defined on measurable time quantities. This is a limitation, but it prevents over-claiming.

