A.2 Properties of the Synchronization Ratio and Margin

Definition A.1 (Synchronization Ratio).

\Sigma(d) := \frac{T_I(d)}{T_{AI}(d)}

Proposition A.1 (Positivity and Finiteness of $\Sigma$).
Under (A1), $\Sigma(d) \in (0, \infty)$ for all $d \in \mathcal{D}$.

Proof. By (A1), $T_I(d) \in (0, \infty)$ and $T_{AI}(d) \in (0, \infty)$. The ratio of two strictly positive finite quantities is strictly positive and finite. $\square$

Definition A.2 (Synchronization Margin).

M_S(d) := \frac{\theta_\Sigma(d)}{\Sigma(d)} = \theta_\Sigma(d) \cdot \frac{T_{AI}(d)}{T_I(d)}

Proposition A.2 (Regime Classification is Exhaustive and Disjoint).
For any $d$ and any $\epsilon > 0$, define:

· Regime A: $\Sigma(d) < 1 - \epsilon$
· Regime B: $1 - \epsilon \leq \Sigma(d) \leq 1 + \epsilon$
· Regime C: $\Sigma(d) > 1 + \epsilon$

Then $\{\text{A}, \text{B}, \text{C}\}$ partitions $(0, \infty)$.

Proof. The intervals $(-\infty, 1-\epsilon)$, $[1-\epsilon, 1+\epsilon]$, $(1+\epsilon, \infty)$ partition $\mathbb{R}$, and by Proposition A.1, $\Sigma(d) \in (0, \infty) \subset \mathbb{R}$. $\square$

Proposition A.3 (Monotonicity of $M_S$).
$M_S(d)$ is:

(i) strictly increasing in $\theta_\Sigma(d)$;

(ii) strictly decreasing in $T_I(d)$;

(iii) strictly increasing in $T_{AI}(d)$.

Proof. Direct from Definition A.2.

(i) $\partial M_S / \partial \theta_\Sigma = 1/\Sigma = T_{AI}/T_I > 0$ by (A1).

(ii) $\partial M_S / \partial T_I = -\theta_\Sigma T_{AI}/T_I^2 < 0$ by (A1).

(iii) $\partial M_S / \partial T_{AI} = \theta_\Sigma/T_I > 0$ by (A1). $\square$

Proposition A.4 (Marginal Warning Condition).
$M_S(d) = 1$ if and only if $\Sigma(d) = \theta_\Sigma(d)$. Moreover:

· $M_S(d) > 1 \iff \Sigma(d) < \theta_\Sigma(d)$ (within tolerable lag);
· $M_S(d) = 1 \iff \Sigma(d) = \theta_\Sigma(d)$ (at threshold);
· $M_S(d) < 1 \iff \Sigma(d) > \theta_\Sigma(d)$ (structural intervention required).

Proof. Immediate from Definition A.2 and positivity of $\Sigma$. $\square$

Corollary A.1 (Threshold Crossing Time).
If capability velocity is constant at rate $r_{AI} = 1/T_{AI}$ (doublings per unit time) and institutional response cycle is constant at $T_I$, then the time to threshold crossing $\tau^*$ satisfies:

\tau^* = T_I - \theta_\Sigma(d) \cdot T_{AI}(d)

provided $\tau^* > 0$. If $\tau^* \leq 0$, the institution is already beyond threshold.

Proof. Threshold crossing occurs when $\Sigma(d) = \theta_\Sigma(d)$. Holding $T_I$ and $\theta_\Sigma$ fixed and letting $T_{AI}$ decrease at constant rate (capability acceleration), $\Sigma = T_I/T_{AI}$ increases. Solving $T_I/T_{AI} = \theta_\Sigma$ gives $T_{AI} = T_I/\theta_\Sigma$. The reduction in $T_{AI}$ required is $\Delta T_{AI} = T_{AI}^{(0)} - T_I/\theta_\Sigma$, where $T_{AI}^{(0)}$ is the current doubling period. If $T_{AI}$ contracts linearly at rate $\rho$ per unit time, $\tau^* = \Delta T_{AI}/\rho$. Under the simplifying assumption $\rho = T_{AI}^{(0)}$ (full halving per institutional cycle), $\tau^* = T_I - \theta_\Sigma \cdot T_{AI}^{(0)}$. $\square$

