A.5 Parity Condition and Its Feasibility

Definition A.5 (Parity Vector and Condition).
Let $\mathbf{P}(d) = (p_1(d), \ldots, p_6(d)) \in [0,1]^6$ and $\boldsymbol{\theta}(d) = (\theta_1(d), \ldots, \theta_6(d)) \in (0,1]^6$. Parity holds iff:

p_i(d) \geq \theta_i(d) \quad \forall i \in \{1, \ldots, 6\}

Proposition A.11 (Parity as Intersection of Half-Spaces).
The parity region $\mathcal{P}(d) = \{\mathbf{p} \in [0,1]^6 : p_i \geq \theta_i \ \forall i\}$ is a hyperrectangle $[\theta_1, 1] \times \cdots \times [\theta_6, 1]$.

Proof. Immediate from the definition. Each inequality defines a closed half-space in $\mathbb{R}^6$; the intersection of axis-aligned half-spaces is a hyperrectangle. $\square$

Proposition A.12 (Monotonic Loss of Parity).
If $p_i(d)$ is decreasing over time for some $i$, then time to loss of parity is:

\tau_i = \inf\{t : p_i(t) < \theta_i\}

System-wide parity loss occurs at $\tau = \min_i \tau_i$.

Proof. Direct from definition of the loss time and the monotonicity assumption. $\square$

Interpretation A.4. Parity is a conjunctive condition: all six dimensions must hold. This is stronger than requiring an average or weighted score. It reflects the design principle that institutional synchronization is a chain of conditions, and failure of any one condition (e.g., verification) compromises the whole. This is the formal basis for the design principle in §4.6 that the parity condition should be vector-valued rather than scalar.

