A.6 Governance Inversion Risk (Formal Sketch)

Definition A.6 (Formalization Complexity).
Let $C_R(t) \geq 0$ denote the formal complexity of an institution's rule set at time $t$ (e.g., number of rules, rule length, or rule dependency depth), and let $C_O(t) \geq 0$ denote the operational complexity of the system it governs.

Definition A.7 (Governance Inversion Risk).
Governance inversion risk is present when:

C_R(t) > \kappa \cdot C_O(t) \quad \text{for some } \kappa > 1

Proposition A.13 (Inversion Risk Condition).
If formal complexity grows superlinearly in operational complexity — i.e., $C_R = f(C_O)$ with $f'' > 0$ and $f(C_O) \geq \kappa C_O$ for $C_O$ above some threshold — then governance inversion risk becomes monotonic in $C_O$ beyond that threshold.

Proof. By convexity, the ratio $f(C_O)/C_O$ is non-decreasing for $C_O > 0$. Thus if $f(C_O^*)/C_O^* \geq \kappa$ at some $C_O^*$, then for all $C_O \geq C_O^*$, $f(C_O)/C_O \geq \kappa$, and inversion risk persists. $\square$

Mitigation Principle A.1. The PAI mitigates inversion risk through:

(i) Capability-triggered review (P4). Rule sets are reviewed when $M_S(d) \leq 1$, not on fixed schedules, so formal complexity is bounded by necessity rather than accretion.

(ii) Algedonic bypass (Eq. 9). When $O_f < \theta_O$, the algedonic channel allows operational decisions to bypass formal rule complexity, restoring operational coherence.

(iii) Vector-valued parity (Eq. 15). Parity is multidimensional, so the institution cannot satisfy its formal obligations on one dimension (e.g., rule count) at the expense of operational effectiveness on others.

Acknowledgment. This formalization is a sketch. Empirical calibration of $\kappa$ and $f$ requires institutional data not yet available (R6).

