Appendix E — Multiplanetary Transition Criteria: Full Derivation

E.1 Population Threshold (500 permanent residents)

Derivation. A settlement requires autonomous governance functions across at least the following essential roles:

Function Minimum staffing Notes
Medical 4 (24/7 coverage) Physician, nurse, medic, pharmacist
Life support 6 (24/7 coverage, 3 shifts) Engineers, technicians
Governance/administration 5 Executive, legislative, judicial, records, elections
Security/emergency 8 (24/7 coverage) Fire, rescue, law enforcement
Food/water 6 Agricultural, water treatment
Manufacturing/maintenance 10 Machinists, fabricators, general maintenance
Education/childcare 6 Multi-generational settlement
Scientific/technical 8 Research, environmental monitoring
Communications/IT 4 Network, data, AI operations
Total essential 57 Minimum viable

With 15% non-working population and 25% redundancy for absence, illness, and rotation, minimum population is approximately:

N_{\min} = \frac{57}{1 - 0.15} \times 1.25 \approx 84

However, this is the bare minimum. For a sustainable, self-governing settlement with cultural continuity, generational replacement, and margin for specialization, historical analogies (small island communities, Antarctic stations with winter-over populations, remote mining towns) suggest 400–600 is the range at which autonomous governance becomes both feasible and desirable. We adopt 500 as the central estimate, acknowledging ±100 uncertainty.

E.2 ISRU Self-Sufficiency (85% closure)

Derivation. Life-support closure is measured as the fraction of mass flows (oxygen, water, food) that are recycled or produced locally rather than imported. Historical benchmarks:

· ISS: ~90% water recovery, ~50% oxygen, ~0% food (all imported).
· Biosphere 2: variable; peak ~80% closure for a short duration.
· NASA Mars reference mission: target 85% closure for long-duration surface missions.

85% closure is the current engineering target for a Mars settlement that can survive a 6-month resupply interruption. Below 85%, the settlement is vulnerable to any supply chain disruption; above 85%, the settlement can sustain operations for extended periods.

E.3 Communication Delay Criterion

Derivation. Earth-Mars one-way light delay varies from approximately 4 minutes (opposition) to 24 minutes (conjunction). Local market clearing time (the time required for economic transactions to complete) in a settlement is on the order of minutes to hours. When communication delay exceeds market clearing time, Earth cannot participate in real-time economic coordination of the settlement, and local institutions must manage economic function autonomously. This criterion is satisfied essentially always for Mars.

E.4 Fiscal Autonomy Index

Definition.

\text{FAI} = \frac{\text{Local revenue}}{\text{Total expenditure}}

Derivation. A settlement with FAI < 0.7 is dependent on external transfers for more than 30% of its expenditure, creating structural dependency. Historical benchmarks for autonomous regions (e.g., Greenland, Faroe Islands) range from 0.5 to 0.8. We adopt 0.7 as the threshold at which an independent fiscal policy becomes feasible.

E.5 Governance Capacity (ACW > 0.6)

Derivation. ACW scores in empirical studies of adaptive governance range from approximately 0.3 (weak) to 0.8 (strong). The 0.6 threshold corresponds to the lower bound of "strong" adaptive capacity in the literature. Below 0.6, the settlement is unlikely to navigate novel crises without external support.

E.6 Consent

Definition. Demonstrated consent requires:

1. Supermajority referendum. At least 2/3 of permanent residents vote in favor of the transition.
2. Exit rights. Residents who do not consent retain the right to return to Earth at collective expense for a defined period.
3. Ongoing consent. Consent is re-demonstrated at each phase transition (e.g., from coordinated but separate to self-governing city-state).
4. Nested councils. Local decisions are made through councils that include representation from all settlement subgroups.

Justification. Consent is the legitimacy condition. Without demonstrated consent, the transition from Earth jurisdiction to autonomy lacks legitimacy, regardless of capacity. This criterion draws on Ostrom's design principles and on democratic theory.

