Article [X] — Transition to Autonomous Governance

Section 1. Threshold Conditions. A settlement shall be eligible for autonomous governance upon demonstration that all of the following conditions are met:

(a) A permanent resident population of at least 500, verified by census conducted under independent supervision;

(b) In-situ resource utilization achieving at least 85% closure for life-support critical mass flows (oxygen, water, food), verified by continuous monitoring for a period of not less than 24 months;

(c) Communication delay between the settlement and Earth exceeding the local market clearing time for a continuous period of not less than 12 months;

(d) A fiscal autonomy index of not less than 0.70, calculated as the ratio of locally generated revenue to total settlement expenditure over a 12-month period;

(e) An Adaptive Capacity Wheel composite score of not less than 0.60 across all six dimensions, assessed by an independent evaluation body;

(f) Demonstrated consent of the resident population, expressed through a referendum in which not less than two-thirds of permanent residents vote in favor, with exit rights guaranteed as specified in Section 4.

Section 2. Recognition. Upon satisfaction of the threshold conditions, the settlement's claim to autonomous governance shall be recognized when a majority of other settlements in the same layer recognize the claim through the reciprocal recognition protocol. Recognition shall not be unreasonably withheld.

Section 3. Residual Earth Authority. Following recognition, Earth-based authorities retain jurisdiction over:

(a) Spectrum allocation and coordination;

(b) Registration of the settlement and its vessels under the Registration Convention (1975);

(c) Rescue obligations under Outer Space Treaty Article V;

(d) Trade standards and interoperability protocols agreed through the Layer 2 coordination protocol.

Section 4. Exit Rights. Any permanent resident who does not consent to autonomous governance retains the right to return to Earth at collective expense for a period of 24 months following recognition. Exit rights shall be exercised without penalty and with full preservation of personal property rights.

Section 5. Ongoing Consent. Consent shall be re-demonstrated at each subsequent phase transition (from coordinated but separate to self-governing city-state) under the same two-thirds supermajority requirement.

Section 6. Sunset of Residual Authority. Residual Earth authority under Section 3 shall sunset upon the earlier of:

(a) A further referendum of the settlement's permanent residents approving full sovereignty with a four-fifths supermajority; or

(b) Achievement of 95% ISRU closure and fiscal autonomy index of 0.90 for 24 continuous months, with recognition by a supermajority of settlements in the layer.

Section 7. Dispute Resolution. Disputes between the settlement and Earth-based authorities, or between the settlement and other settlements, shall be resolved through the polycentric dispute resolution mechanism specified in Appendix [Y], incorporating the design principles of clearly defined boundaries, congruence with local conditions, collective-choice arrangements, monitoring, graduated sanctions, conflict-resolution mechanisms, recognition of rights to organize, and nested enterprises.

